const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What does it mean for precast to be specified?",
    a: "It means precast is written into the project's construction documents as the required system, usually in a spec section such as 03 41 00 for structural precast or 03 45 00 for architectural precast. The spec may name acceptable manufacturers, name one producer as the basis of design, or set certification requirements that only some plants can meet. Once precast is in the spec, general contractors bidding the job must price it, and producers who fit the spec get to quote. Producers who were never findable during design rarely appear in that section.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do architects find precast producers?",
    a: "Early in design, they search the way anyone searches: a browser, a finish they saw on another building, a rep they already know, or a colleague's recommendation. What they land on is your website, and what they judge is your photography and your finish options. If the photos are thin or the finishes page is vague, they move to the next producer without telling you. The producers who get sampled are the ones whose built work an architect could see.",
  },
  {
    q: "What should a precast producer put on its website?",
    a: "Four things, in order: professional photographs of finished, installed work; a finishes and capabilities page specific enough that a specifier can shortlist you from it; typical details, span tables, and product data a designer can download without asking; and a phone number that reaches a person. Everything else is secondary. A specifier on a deadline is looking for reasons to keep you in or cut you, and the site either supplies them or it does not.",
  },
  {
    q: "What does precast marketing cost?",
    a: "We work on a single retainer scoped to the actual work, with no hourly billing. The number depends on what needs fixing: a producer who needs photography and a rebuilt finishes page is a different scope than one who needs the whole chain covered, from findability through bid-time support. The honest answer comes from a scope conversation, which costs nothing and usually tells you within an hour what the work looks like.",
  },
];
